<BaseLayout title="MDR: Motor Driven Roller Conveyors | 24VDC Zone Control Guide" description="MDR (motor driven roller) conveyors put 24VDC motors inside rollers for zone control and zero-pressure accumulation. MDR vs line-shaft vs belt-driven compared." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>MDR: Motor Driven Roller Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">An MDR (Motor Driven Roller) conveyor uses small brushless DC motors, most commonly 24VDC, built inside individual conveyor rollers to create independently controlled zones for product transport and zero-pressure accumulation. MDR is widely used for new powered roller lines in distribution, fulfillment, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a> applications because it removes the central gearmotor, drive belt or chain, and compressed air that conventional <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller conveyors</a> need for accumulation. Zones run only when product is present, which cuts energy use compared to conveyors that run continuously.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How MDR Technology Works</h2>
<h3>The Motorized Roller</h3>
<p>An MDR roller looks like a standard conveyor roller from the outside: a steel or stainless tube with bearings and axles. Inside, a compact brushless DC (BLDC) motor and planetary gearbox turn the outer tube. No external gearmotor, belt, or chain exists. The drive fits inside a standard 1.9-inch class roller tube.</p>
<p>Speed and torque depend on the gear ratio selected. <a href="https://www.interroll.com/fileadmin/products/product_data/EC-5000-IP54/EC5000_50mm_IP54_USA.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Interroll's EC5000 RollerDrive datasheet</a> (1.9-inch, IP54) lists 24V and 48V versions at 20, 35, and 50 W, with nine gear ratios spanning maximum conveying speeds from 32 fpm (108:1) to 382 fpm (9:1). Low ratios give speed, high ratios give torque. Rated current at 24V is 1.4 A (20 W) to 3.4 A (50 W). Itoh Denki lists a 7 to 700 FPM speed range for its <a href="https://www.itohdenki.com/pm486fe" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PM486FE Power Moller</a>, depending on model. <a href="https://www.rulmeca.com/en/products_unit/catalogue/10/rdr_rulmeca_drive_roller/18/rulmeca_drive_rollers_rdr" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rulmeca's RDR drive roller</a> is a third 24VDC motorized roller platform.</p>
<h3>Zone Architecture</h3>
<p>Each MDR zone has one motorized roller, a set of slave rollers driven from it by O-rings or belts, one photo-eye sensor, and one zone controller. The zone controller reads the sensor, communicates with adjacent zone controllers, and controls the motor roller's run, stop, speed, and direction.</p>
<p>Zone length is chosen from the supplier's standard zone lengths so that each zone holds one product. Size it to the longest product you will accumulate. Hytrol's <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/E24.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">E24 product literature</a> offers 18-, 24-, 30-, and 36-inch zones on its 190-E24EZ, with 1.9-inch rollers on 3-inch centers. Each zone runs, stops, or reverses independently. This independent zone control is the foundation of <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">zero-pressure accumulation</a>.</p>
<h3>Zero-Pressure Accumulation Logic</h3>
<p>MDR ZPA uses a cascading signal between zones. Each zone has three states: empty (no product detected), occupied-and-running (product present, downstream clear), and occupied-and-stopped (product present, downstream occupied). When a zone sees "downstream occupied," it stops its motor. The product stops in that zone with a gap to the product ahead. The signal cascades upstream, filling zones back to front with gaps between products.</p>
<p>When the blockage clears, the leading zone starts first, then the next zone, and the release cascades upstream. Products leave in the order they arrived (first in, first out).</p>
<h3>24VDC Power Distribution</h3>
<p>MDR systems distribute 24VDC from power supplies to each zone. Supply count depends on motor wattage and zone count. Hytrol's <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/BZDE24EZN.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BZDE24EZN specification sheet</a>, for example, allows up to 10 of its 48 W motors, or up to 8 of its 60 W motors, on one 40 amp supply. Low-voltage DC wiring can reduce the amount of line-voltage wiring run to individual conveyor sections. Confirm wiring methods and conduit requirements with your electrical engineer and local code.</p>
<h2 id="comparison-heading">MDR vs Line-Shaft vs Belt-Driven</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>MDR</th>
<th>Belt-Driven (BDLR)</th>
<th>Line-Shaft</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Drive Method</td>
<td>24VDC motor inside roller</td>
<td>AC gearmotor + belt</td>
<td>AC gearmotor + shaft + O-rings</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Zone Control</td>
<td>Built-in per zone</td>
<td>Add-on (typically pneumatic)</td>
<td>Add-on (clutch/brake)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ZPA Capability</td>
<td>Native</td>
<td>With belt lifters</td>
<td>With O-ring release</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Load Rating</td>
<td>Model-specific. Hytrol 190-E24EZ: 75 lbs per zone</td>
<td>Model-specific, generally higher than roller MDR</td>
<td>Model-specific, light loads</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Energy Use</td>
<td>Zones run only when product is present</td>
<td>Central motor runs continuously</td>
<td>Central motor runs continuously</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Noise</td>
<td>Low. Interroll EC5000: 55 dB(A) max installed, application-dependent</td>
<td>Central drive and belt noise</td>
<td>Central drive and shaft noise</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Compressed Air</td>
<td>Not required</td>
<td>Required for pneumatic zones</td>
<td>Depends on zone method</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Key Maintenance</td>
<td>Replace motor roller or zone card</td>
<td>Belt, gearmotor, air components</td>
<td>O-rings, gearmotor</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Main Cost Drivers</td>
<td>One motor roller and controller per zone, power supplies</td>
<td>Central drive, drive belt, pneumatic zone hardware and air supply</td>
<td>Central drive, line shaft, O-rings, zone hardware</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>MDR usually costs more per foot upfront because every zone carries its own motor and controller. Lower energy use and fewer central drive components can offset that over the system's life. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Energy savings depend on duty cycle.</strong> Insight Automation, which sells PULSEROLLER motorized roller conveyors, <a href="https://www.pulseroller.com/resources/pulsenews/motorized_roller_conveyors_a_better_option_for_small_and_medium_sized_businesses" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">estimates</a> that rollers in a typical section run 10 to 50 percent of operating time, which it equates to 30 to 70 percent energy savings versus conveyors that run continuously. Treat this as a vendor estimate. Your savings depend on how often product is actually present on each zone.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">MDR Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Distribution and fulfillment:</strong> Totes, shipping cartons, and polybag mailers route through pick modules, merge onto trunk lines, and <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulate</a> before shipping sorters. Low noise matters in facilities where long runs of conveyor operate near workers.</p>
<p><strong>E-commerce order processing:</strong> MDR handles a mixed product stream (poly bags, padded envelopes, small boxes) with ZPA that prevents damage during accumulation. Very small or soft items may need belted zones instead of bare rollers. <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">Automated conveyor systems</a> integrate MDR zones with barcode scanners, print-and-apply labelers, and <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Packaging line accumulation:</strong> MDR provides clean ZPA for <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a>. Cases, cartons, and shrink-wrapped packs queue between fillers, labelers, case packers, and palletizers without back-pressure damage, and without the pneumatic infrastructure that belt-driven accumulation requires.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage:</a></strong> Washdown-rated motor rollers exist. Itoh Denki offers IP65 waterproof and IP55 drip-proof options on the PM486FE, and Interroll publishes an <a href="https://www.interroll.com/fileadmin/user_upload/Downloads__PDF_/Drives___Controls/DC_Platform/EC5000_Catalog_PDF/US/EC5000_IP66.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IP66 EC5000 version</a> for areas cleaned with water jets. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary MDR systems</a> pair sealed motors with open frames. The absence of exposed central drive belts and chains simplifies cleaning.</p>
<p><strong>Before <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfers</a> and merges:</strong> MDR zones upstream of pop-up transfers, merges, and sortation diverters hold each product at a known position and release it on command. This removes the spacing variability that causes transfer jams on continuous-run conveyors.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing applications:</a></strong> MDR zones provide stop-and-go indexing for robotic pick-and-place, vision inspection, labeling, and printing stations. Braking features help positioning: Interroll lists an electronic holding brake on the EC5000, and Itoh Denki lists a dynamic brake on the PM486FE.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">When to Choose MDR</h2>
<p><strong>Choose MDR when:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>You need zero-pressure accumulation without pneumatic infrastructure</li>
<li>Product weight fits the conveyor's per-zone and per-foot rating (check the specific model's sheet)</li>
<li>Energy use matters and the line has frequent idle periods</li>
<li>Low noise is important</li>
<li>You want no central drive belt, chain, or line shaft to maintain</li>
<li>You need easy future expansion (add zones without new central motors or air)</li>
<li>Low-voltage DC distribution fits your electrical plan</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Choose <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">belt-driven live roller</a> instead when:</strong> product weight exceeds the MDR model's zone rating, you need speeds the MDR gearing cannot reach at the required torque, or upfront cost is the main constraint and you do not need accumulation.</p>
<p><strong>Choose <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a> instead when:</strong> you handle pallets, drums, or other heavy loads, need positive chain drive with no slip, or <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet handling</a> is the primary application. 24-volt chain-driven zone conveyors also exist: Hytrol rates its 199-CRE24EZ at up to 600 lbs per zone.</p>
<p><strong>Choose <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller</a> instead when:</strong> no powered transport is needed, a slight decline is available, and budget is the top constraint.</p>
<h2 id="mfg-heading">How to Evaluate MDR Options</h2>
<p>The MDR supply chain has two tiers: component makers (who build the motorized rollers) and conveyor manufacturers or integrators (who build complete systems). Motorized roller makers include Interroll (RollerDrive), Itoh Denki (Power Moller), and Rulmeca (RDR). Some conveyor manufacturers use their own 24-volt drives, such as Hytrol's E24 motor with EZLogic controls.</p>
<p>Ask each supplier for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Motor roller make, model, wattage, and gear ratio, with the datasheet</li>
<li>Rated load per zone and per foot for the exact conveyor model</li>
<li>Available zone lengths versus your longest and shortest product</li>
<li>Zone controller type and accumulation modes (singulation, slug release, workstation stop)</li>
<li>Network interface to your PLC (discrete I/O or industrial Ethernet)</li>
<li>Power supply sizing: motors per supply and supply location</li>
<li>IP rating and temperature range for your environment</li>
<li>Spare parts list and motor roller replacement procedure</li>
</ul>
