<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What does MDR stand for?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>MDR stands for Motor Driven Roller. A brushless DC motor, most commonly 24VDC, sits inside the roller tube, creating an independent drive zone. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor hub</a> for all roller types.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What are the advantages over belt-driven roller?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Built-in ZPA without compressed air, lower energy use because zones run only when product is present (Insight Automation estimates 30 to 70 percent savings versus continuously running conveyors), quieter operation (Interroll lists 55 dB(A) maximum installed for its EC5000, application-dependent), no central drive belt or chain to maintain, and easier expansion. Upfront cost is usually higher because each zone has its own motor and controller. See the <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller comparison</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What weight can MDR handle?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>It depends on the conveyor model. Hytrol rates its 190-E24EZ at 37 lbs per foot and 75 lbs per zone. Interroll's 1.9-inch EC5000 roller carries up to 242 lbs static (up to 39 inches long, without drive head). For heavier loads, use 24-volt chain-driven zones (Hytrol 199-CRE24EZ: up to 600 lbs per zone) or <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How does MDR zone control work?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Each zone has one motorized roller, several slave rollers, a photo-eye sensor, and a zone controller card. The controller reads the sensor and communicates with adjacent zones. When downstream is occupied, the upstream zone holds its product with a gap, creating <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">zero-pressure accumulation</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">Who manufactures MDR rollers?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Interroll (RollerDrive), Itoh Denki (Power Moller), and Rulmeca (RDR) make motorized rollers. Conveyor manufacturers and integrators build them, or their own 24-volt drives such as Hytrol's E24, into complete systems.</p>

</div>
</div>
